SRC_002 is internally inconsistent: it states that obstruction prevalence scales like n^{1/2+o(1)} but then concludes P(consensus)=1−Θ(1). If the expected number of obstructions is n^{1/2+o(1)} with variance of the same order, the probability of at least one obstruction cannot be Θ(1); under the claimed Poisson-like fluctuations it should tend to 1. The product Θ(n^{1/2})·Θ(n^{-1/2}) treats n^{1/2} as the number of frontier opportunities, not as the prevalence of obstructions, so the target equivocates between opportunity count and obstruction count. This makes the quantitative law non-falsifiable as stated.

